# WHAT A RELEASE SHIPS.
#
# `git archive` — which is what GitHub runs to produce the tarball attached to a
# tag — honours `export-ignore`. This file is therefore the difference between
# what the REPOSITORY carries and what a RELEASE carries, and the two should not
# be the same thing.
#
# The repository carries its evidence on purpose: 977 of 1,598 tracked files are
# campaign results under `evidence/`, and they are there so that every number
# in `docs/reports/` can be recomputed rather than believed. That is the right
# call for the repository and the wrong one for a tarball, where it is 61% of
# the file count and 0% of the use.
#
# The rule for adding a line here: would someone who wants to USE Druse be worse
# off without this path? If not, it is not part of a release.

# Campaign evidence: every run behind every number in docs/reports/.
evidence/           export-ignore

# The owner's working area — labs, delivery bundles, the session dossier. None
# of it is tracked today; the line is here so a stray commit cannot put it in a
# release by accident.
PRIORIDADE/         export-ignore

# Planning, freeze manifests, ADRs, risk register, roadmaps. The record of how
# decisions were made; not needed to use what they decided.
planning/           export-ignore
knowledge-base/     export-ignore
release-candidate/  export-ignore

# Throwaway prototypes. They ratify signatures; they are not the framework.
experiments/        export-ignore

# The gate, its mutation controls, and the benchmark harnesses. Everything here
# is for developing Druse, not for depending on it.
build/              export-ignore
bench/              export-ignore
ops/                export-ignore
tests/              export-ignore

# Working copies and agent scratch.
bundle/             export-ignore
tina/               export-ignore
.claude/            export-ignore
.githooks/          export-ignore
.gitattributes      export-ignore
.gitignore          export-ignore

# WHAT REMAINS, and it is the whole of the answer to "how do I use this":
#   web/          the library
#   vendor/       the pinned backend it needs to build
#   examples/     compiling programs
#   docs/         the guide, reference, cookbook, operations and error contract
#   README.md CHANGELOG.md LICENSE SECURITY.md CONTRIBUTING.md
#   odin-version.txt   the toolchain the above is pinned to
